          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Kinship
            is the collection of relations, rules and behaviors concerning alliance and fictive or real consanguinity that occur in the social organisation of the biological reproduction of individuals. Kinship relations play a significant role for individuals as well as for society as a whole.
          %p
            In particular in pre-industrial societies, systems of marriage and kinship are often cast into rigid and intricate patterns that are a preferred playground for applications of mathematics to anthropology ever since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Weil"} André Weil
            contributed an algebraic appendix to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claude+L%C3%A9vi-Strauss"} Claude Lévi-Strauss'
            groundbreaking
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LS67"}
                %em Les structures élémentaires de la parenté
          %h2#permutation_models Permutation models
          %p It is generally assumed that all societies explicitly impose some restrictions on the types of possible marriages. The resulting kinship systems then differ whether these restrictions take on the form of prohibitions like e.g. in modern societies that merely ban certain marriages due to considerations of consanguinity or age, or the form of prescriptions that specify positively to marry into a determinate ‘marriage class’ or category of kins.
          %p
            The latter prescriptive systems have been called
            %em elementary structures
            by Lévi-Strauss and further divided into systems of
            %em restricted exchange
            where two groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
            directly exchange spouses, or systems of
            %em generalized exchange
            with longer cycles of exchange e.g. group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            gives spouses to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            that gives spouses to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            that gives spouses to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            Since marriages become deterministic on the classes, elementary kinship systems are amenable to analysis via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutations"} permutations
            on the set of marriage classes. This group-theoretic modeling of elementary structures has been pioneered by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Weil"} André Weil
            (
            %a{:href => "#Weil49"}> 1949
            ). Here is Lévi-Strauss on Weil’s contribution:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Historiquement parlant, ces pages ont une grande importance. Toute la mathématique de la parenté qui s’est beaucoup développée depuis en est sortie. Et cela continue.
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p Weil’s ideas have later been systematized by Philippe Courrège and others.
          #kinship.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              An
              %em (elementary) kinship system
              is a triple
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\omega,\mu)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is a finite set, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega,\mu
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutations"} permutations
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %p
              A
              %em morphism of kinship systems
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S_1,\omega_1,\mu_1)\to (S_2,\omega_2,\mu_2)
              is a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:S_1\to S_2
              that is equivariant with respect to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              i.e.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ\omega_1 =\omega_2\circ f
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi μ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi μ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ\mu_1 =\mu_2\circ f
            %p
              The resulting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              is called the
              = succeed "." do
                %em category of kinship (systems)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The axiomatics was proposed in
              %a{:href => "#Cour65"} Courrège (1965)
              with the intended interpretation that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              represents the
              = succeed "," do
                %em set of (disjoint) marriage classes
              %em conjugal
              function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              links the men in class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              to the class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega (x)
              that contains their wifes (’
              %em>
                men in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                marry women in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega (x)
              ’) and that the
              %em matrilineal
              function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              links the mothers in class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              to the class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu (x)
              that contains their children (’
              %em>
                women in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                have children in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(x)
              ’).
            %p
              One can then define a
              %em patrilineal
              function
              %sup#fnref:2
                %a{:href => "#fn:2", :rel => "footnote"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo :
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo =
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =\mu\circ\omega
              that links the fathers in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              to their children in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\omega (x))
              (’
              %em>
                men in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                have children in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\omega (x))
              ’).
            %p
              Since a kinship structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\omega,\mu)
              contains an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              of ‘
              %em> alliance
              ’ and an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              of ‘
              %em> descent
              ’ one could view the axiomatics as a reconciliation of the Anglo-Saxon tradition in kinship studies that stressed descent and Lévi-Strauss’ views that stressed alliance. But this ‘reconciliation’ is more one of appearances as one could equally well use
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              as undefined terms and define
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi μ
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega =\mu^{-1}\circ \pi
          #kinship.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given a system of kinship
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ,
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\omega,\mu)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Aut
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(S)
              generated by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
              is called the
              %em group of kinship terms
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ,
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\omega,\mu)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,\omega,\mu)
              is called
              %em irreducible
              (resp.
              = succeed ")" do
                %em regular
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is
              %em transitive
              (resp.
              = succeed ")" do
                %em free
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  For all
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y\in S
                  there is a
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\in G
                  such that
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g x=y
              %li
                %p
                  (resp. For all
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi G
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\in G
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ≠
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\neq 1
                  then
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≠
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g x\neq x
                  for all
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi S
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in S
          %p
            To equip a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with a kinship structure amounts to give a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:\{1,2\}\to |Aut(S)|
            that picks out
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(1)=\omega
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(2)=\mu
            from the set |Aut(S)| of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphisms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\dashv |\cdot|
            between the free-group functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:Set\to Grp
            and the underlying-set functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo :
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\cdot|: Grp\to Set
            this is the same as giving a group homorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: F(\{1,2\})\to Aut(S)
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free group
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{1,2\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
            in the following, to the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(S)
            of automorphisms of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
            on the set
            = succeed "!" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            The group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of kinship terms is none other than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi im
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Aut
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\alpha)\subseteq Aut(S)
          %p
            As this goes through regardless of whether
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is finite or not and makes sense with respect to the morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
            we have
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
              of kinship systems is equivalent to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              of finite
              = succeed "-sets" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+group"} free group
              on two generators.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            Hence, the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
            has a rich mathematical structure and naturally admits not only finite products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ×
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\times T
            that play an important role already in
            %a{:href => "#Cour65"} Courrège (1965)
            but also more ‘exotic’ operations like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponentials
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^S
            and, in particular, power systems
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^S
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is the truth-value object or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            which, in the case of a topos of group actions for a group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{G}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\emptyset , \tilde{G}\}\cong\{0,1\}
            with trivial action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\cdot \tilde{g}=0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\cdot \tilde{g}=1
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo ∈
                    %mover
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{g}\in \tilde{G}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
            the truth-value object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mi id
                  %mo ,
                  %mi id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega =(\{0,1\}, id, id)
            is a system with two classes with trivial structure. In fact,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega = \mathbf{1}\times \mathbf{1}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mi id
                  %mo ,
                  %mi id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}=(\{1\},id,id)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
          %p
            An
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functor"} flat functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T:F_2\to Set
            which by definition amounts to a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int _{F_2} T
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+category"} filtered
          %p (…)
          %h3#some_terminology_and_properties Some terminology and properties
          %p
            A system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ,
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}=(S,\omega,\mu)\in \mathcal{K}
            is called
            %em exogamic
            when
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega\neq id
            %em endogamic
            otherwise. This simply says that spouses always stem from the same marriage class (resp. from different classes).
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
            is called
            %em matrilineal
            (resp.
            = succeed ")" do
              %em patrilineal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo =
                  %mi id
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu = id
            (resp.
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi = id
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
            is called a
            %em system of restricted exchange
            if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega^2=id
            %em generalized exchange
            otherwise.
          %p
            A particular important category of kin are
            %em cousins
            e.g. in many Western societies they are the closest type of kins eligible for marriage whereas in Arabian societies patrilateral parallel cousin marriage is often the preferred type of marriage.
          %p This can be viewed as a consequence of the property that the cousin category is at the intersection of important poles of kinship relations as affinity and non-affinity, symmetry and asymmetry (in the case of cross-cousins e.g. the father’sister’s daughter from male perspective and the mother’s brother’s son in reverse direction). This warrants
          #cousin.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A system
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}=(S,\omega,\mu)\in \mathcal{K}
              is said to
              %em admit marriage with the (female) matrilateral cross-cousin
              if
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mi μ
                      %mo =
                      %mi μ
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega\mu = \mu\omega
                (MCC)
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
              is said to
              %em admit marriage with the (female) patrilateral cross-cousin
              if
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mi μ
                      %mo =
                      %mi μ
                      %msup
                        %mi ω
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega\mu = \mu\omega^{-1}
                (PCC)
          %p A direct calculation yields
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}=(S,\omega,\mu)\in \mathcal{K}
              satisfy MCC.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
              satifies PCC iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
              is of restricted exchange.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            generate the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of kinship terms the following is immediate
          #commutative.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              A system
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒦
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}=(S,\omega,\mu)\in \mathcal{K}
              satisfies MCC iff the group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              of kinship terms of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
              is commutative.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qed
          %p
            In fact, the fullsubcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}\subset \mathcal{K}
            of kinships systems satisfying MCC is equivalent to the topos of
            = succeed "-actions," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}
            is the free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abelian+group"} Abelian group
            on two generators because actions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is commutative correspond precisely to actions of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}
          %p
            A more general way to view this, is to consider the surjective homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha:F_2\to \mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}
            viewed as a functor that sends the generators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1,2
            to the generators
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,0),(0,1)
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#MM94"} MM94, p.359
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#RRZ04"} RRZ04, p.226
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential geometric morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi α
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo
                          %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℤ
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\alpha_!\dashv \alpha^*\dashv\alpha_*:Set^{F_2{^{op}}}\to Set^{(\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z})^{op}}
          %p (…)
          %h2#topostheoretic_perspectives Topos-theoretic perspectives
          %p
            In our exposition above we have already taken advantage of the categorical point of view advanced in Lawvere and Schanuel (
            %a{:href => "#LawSchan97"}> 1997
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Law99"} 1999
          %p
            Lawvere and Schanuel proposed to study the concept of kinship starting from the notion of a (filiation structured)
            %strong society
            modeled mathematically as a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (of
            = succeed ")" do
              %em individuals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,f
            that assign to an individual
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            his mother
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(x)\in X
            resp. his father
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)\in X
          %p
            If one organizes these sets with filiation structure into a category the result is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            namely the topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M_2^{op}}
            of right actions of the free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
            on two symbols
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{m,f\}
          %p
            Since the parental endofunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,f
            are completely unconstrained
            %sup#fnref:3>
              %a{:href => "#fn:3", :rel => "footnote"} 3
            , the resulting ‘kinship relations’ are equally unconstrained, e.g. nothing prevents an individual’s mother from coinciding with the same individual’s father etc. It therefore becomes advantageous to equip the ‘societies’ with further structure. Lawvere and Schanuel propose to achieve this by introducing appropriate labeling objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and passage to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi M
                          %mn 2
                          %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M_2^{op}}/L
          %p
            A minimal model of a society that at least keeps the
            %strong gender
            of the parents apart is the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi female
                  %mo ,
                  %mi male
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G=\{female, male\}
            consisting of a ‘female’ and a distinct ‘male’ individual
            %sup#fnref:4
              %a{:href => "#fn:4", :rel => "footnote"} 4
            together with parental functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,f
            complying with the usual gender rules e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi female
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi female
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(female)=female
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi female
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi male
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(female)=male
            etc. Then a consistent gender distinction on the parental structure of an arbitrary society corresponds to a structure preserving map to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            i.e. an object of the slice topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi M
                          %mn 2
                          %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M_2^{op}}/G
          %p
            Similarly,
            %strong exogamy
            can be modeled by a
            %em clans object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            e.g. consisting of two clans
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi bear
                  %mo ,
                  %mi wolf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=\{bear, wolf\}
            obeying matrilinearity in the sense that the mother-of function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            is constant but the father-of function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            switches the clans expressing thereby that the father of an individual comes from another clan than the individual and his mother.
            %sup#fnref:5
              %a{:href => "#fn:5", :rel => "footnote"} 5
          %p
            By labeling with the product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times C
            one can achieve gender distinction and exogamy at the same time:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi he
                          %mo −
                          %mi wolf
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %munder
                              %mo ⇆
                              %mi f
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi he
                          %mo −
                          %mi bear
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo
                            %mi m
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo
                            %mi f
                          %mo ↗
                          %mo ↖
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mo
                              %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mo
                              %mi m
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mrow
                              %mi she
                              %mo −
                              %mi wolf
                            %mover
                              %mi m
                              %mo ↺
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mrow
                              %mi she
                              %mo −
                              %mi bear
                            %mover
                              %mi m
                              %mo ↺
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G\times C \quad=\qquad\array{
                  he-wolf &amp;\overset{f}{\underset{f}{\leftrightarrows}}&amp; he-bear\\
                  _{m}\downarrow&amp;_{f}{\nearrow}  {\nwarrow}{_{f}}&amp; \downarrow{_{m}}\\
                  \\underset{\overset{\circlearrowleft}{m}}{she-wolf}&amp; &amp;\underset{\overset{\circlearrowleft}{m}}{she-bear}
                  }\quad
          %p
            Occasionally, one can exploit the “biological unreasonability” inherent in the unconstrained parental mappings
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,f
            %strong fatherless society
            lacking marriage or social acknowledgement of male contribution to procreation (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Godelier04"} Godelier, pp.494-509
          %p
            The society consists of matrilineal families consisting of a woman, her brothers and her children. Conception takes place via “nocturnal male visitors” from other houses. In first approximation we can model this absence of fatherhood by the triviality of the father-of-map
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=id
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is his own father. This corresponds to the full subcategory of societies of form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,m:X\to X, id_X)
            which is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M_1^{op}}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_1
            is the free monoid generated by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{m\}
          %p
            This subcategory is the image under the full and faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^*
            arising from the adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                        %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi M
                        %mn 1
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_!\dashv q^*\dashv q_*: Set^{M_2^{op}}\to Set^{M_1^{op}}
          %p
            induced by the surjective morphism of monoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:M_2\to M_1
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(f)=1
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(m)=m
          %p
            Even societies that entertain an elaborate pattern of kinship relations and terminologies tend to blur distinctions with increasing genealogical depth e.g. whereas parallel and cross kin differ terminologically in the parental generation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{+1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{+2}
            of the grandparents. This lumping together of generations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{\leq i}
            to a cluster of ‘ancestors’ is achieved by quotienting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
            with relations expressing the equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mi j
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi j
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^{+j}\cong G^{+(j+1)}
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\leq j
            e.g. for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=2
            this amounts to equate all expressions of depth
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            with an appropriate expression of depth
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mn 3
                  %mo =
                  %mi fmf
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^3=fmf=f^2
            etc. The resulting epimorphism of monoids
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo ≈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2\to M_\approx
            induces functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi α
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo
                          %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi M
                            %mo ≈
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_!\dashv \alpha^*\dashv\alpha_*:Set^{M_2{^{op}}}\to Set^{{M_\approx}^{op}}
            like above.
          %p
            At the end of his
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Law99"} paper
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+general"} abstract general
            not the category corresponding to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
            but the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            based on the diagram of three parallel arrows
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                  %munder
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bullet{\underset{\rightarrow}{\overset{\rightarrow}{\rightarrow}}}\bullet
          %p
            The result still contains the topos of
            = succeed "-actions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{C}^{op}}
            permit a more finely grained analysis than the objects in the topos of
            = succeed "-actions." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
          %h3#some_further_remarks Some further remarks
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %msubsup
                            %mi M
                            %mn 2
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M_2^{op}}
                lacks non-trivial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level"} essential subtoposes
                since by an result of Kelly-Lawvere (
                %a{:href => "#KL89"}> 1989
                ) they correspond to two-sided (=left+right) ideals
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_2
                that are
                %em idempotent
                i.e.
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi I
                        %mo =
                        %mi I
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi I
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi xy
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I=I\cdot I=\{xy|x,y\in I\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                has a minimal length
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo #
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} #I
                such that there is a word
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi w
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w\in I
                of length
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo #
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} #I
                but no word
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v\in I
                of strictly smaller length. Since concatenation with non-empty words strictly increases the length of a word, one has
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo #
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo #
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} #(I\cdot I)=2\cdot #I
                hence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo #
                      %mi I
                      %mo ≠
                      %mo #
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} #I\neq #(I\cdot I)
                iff
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo ∉
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon\notin I
                and, accordingly,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi I
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\neq I\cdot I
                iff
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo ∉
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon\notin I
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%B3nsson-Tarski+topos"} Jónsson-Tarski topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒥
                        %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{J}_T
                is a (non-essential)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtopos
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Set
                          %mrow
                            %msubsup
                              %mi M
                              %mn 2
                              %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M_2^{op}}
            %li
              %p
                The construction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi mod
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\mathsf{mod}A
                that
                %a{:href => "#Law99"} Lawvere (1999)
                discusses p.417, is defined as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
                of the inclusion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\hookrightarrow X
                along the map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to\Pi_0(A)
                that maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in A
                to its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+component"} connected component
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a]\in\Pi_0(A)
                (where the set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0(A)
                of connected components of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is equipped with the trivial functions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo =
                      %mi id
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega=id
                and
                = succeed "):" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mo =
                        %mi id
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu=id
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi A
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↪
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi Π
                                %mn 0
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi A
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↪
                            %mtd
                              %mi X
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                                %mi mod
                              %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\begin{matrix}
                      A &amp;\hookrightarrow &amp; X \\
                      \\downarrow &amp;          &amp;\downarrow\\
                      \\Pi_0(A) &amp;\hookrightarrow  &amp; X\mathsf{mod} A
                      \\end{matrix}
            %li
              %p
                The categories of the form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W/G
                occurring on p.420 and p.424 denote the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi W
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int _{W} G
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mo :
                        %msup
                          %mi W
                          %mi op
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G:W^{op}\to Set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+topos"} slice topos
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝒞
                            %mi op
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\mathcal{C}^{op}}/X
                is equivalent to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi 𝒞
                          %mi X
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{(\int _{\mathcal{C}}X)^{op}}
                (see at
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
                for more details). Whence labeling with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                can equivalently be achieved by taking presheaves over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi W
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int _W L
                \!
            %li
              %p
                The construction of Grothendieck that permits internalising a relative age functor mentioned on p.416 is given by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements#ColimitPreserving2"} right adjoint to the category of elements
                construction (cf.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+stacks"} Pursuing stacks
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claude+L%C3%A9vi-Strauss"} Claude Lévi-Strauss
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+formula+of+myth"} canonical formula of myth
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structuralism"} structuralism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicategory"} quasicategory
          %h2#links Links
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia entry: kinship (
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinship"}> link
                )
            %li
              %p
                Mathematical Anthropology and Cultural Theory (
                %a{:href => "http://mathematicalanthropology.org"}> online journal
                )
          %h2#references References
          %p Early pioneering attempts include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Alexander Macfarlane,
                %em Analysis of relationships of consanguinity and affinity
                , J. Royal Anthr. Inst.
                %strong 12
                (1882) pp.46-63.
            %li
              %p
                John H. Greenberg,
                %em The logical analysis of kinship
                , Philosophy of Science
                %strong 15
                (1949) pp.58-64.
            %li
              %p
                Rudolf Carnap,
                %em Introduction to Symbolic Logic and its Applications
                , Dover New York 1958. (pp.220-225, 230)
          %p An obstinate 70s’ rumour has it that ‘kinship’ boils down to a Western folk myth. Already better informed:
          %ul
            %li
              Ernest Gellner,
              %em The concept of kinship
              , Philosophy of Science
              %strong 27
              (1960) pp.187–204.
          %p A comprehensive and highly recommendable anthology is
          %ul
            %li#PS04
              Robert Parkin, Linda Stone (eds.),
              %em Kinship and Family
              , Blackwell Oxford 2004.
          %p A conspicuously clear and concise introduction can be found in
          %ul
            %li#Op09
              Michael Oppitz,
              %em Notwendige Beziehungen - Abriß der strukturalen Anthropologie
              , Suhrkamp Frankfurt am Main 2009².
          %p Current textbooks include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Robert Parkin,
                %em Kinship: An Introduction to the Basic Concepts
                , Blackwell Oxford 1997.
            %li
              %p
                Linda Stone,
                %em Kinship and Gender: An Introduction
                , Westview 2013.
            %li
              %p
                Murray J. Leaf, Dwight Read,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to the Science of Kinship
          %p Classical anthropological texts are
          %ul
            %li#Fox83
              %p
                Robin Fox,
                %em Kinship and Marriage
                , 2nd ed. Cambridge UP 1983.
            %li#LS67
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claude+L%C3%A9vi-Strauss"}> Claude Lévi-Strauss
                ,
                %em Les structures élémentaires de la parenté
                , 2nd ed. Mouton The Hague 1967.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claude+L%C3%A9vi-Strauss"}> Claude Lévi-Strauss
                ,
                %em Réflexions sur l’atome de parenté
                , L’Homme
                %strong 13
                no.3 (1973) pp.5-30. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.persee.fr/doc/hom_0439-4216_1973_num_13_3_367355"}> link
                )
          %p More recent texts include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                German Dziebel,
                %em The Genius of Kinship
                , Cambria Youngstown 2007.
            %li#Godelier04
              %p
                Maurice Godelier,
                %em Métamorphoses de la parenté
                , Fayard Paris 2004.
            %li#GTT98
              %p
                Maurice Godelier, Thomas R. Trautmann, Franklin E. Tjon Sie Fat (eds.),
                %em Transformations of Kinship
                , Smithsonian Institute Washington 1998.
          %p The permutation model originates with
          %ul
            %li#Weil49
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Weil"}> André Weil
              ,
              %em Sur l’étude algébrique de certains types de lois de mariage (Système Murngin)
              , pp.257-263 in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#LS67"} Lévi-Strauss (1949/1967)
          %p A review of Weil’s approach as well as an overview of mathematical approaches to kinship is provided by
          %ul
            %li
              Pin-Hsiung Liu,
              %em Theory of groups of permutations, matrices and kinship: a critique of mathematical approaches to prescriptive marriage systems
              , Bulletin of the Institute of Ethnology Academia Sinica
              %strong 26
              (1968) pp.29–38. (
              %a{:href => "http://c.ianthro.tw/sites/c.ianthro.tw/files/da/df/407/407386_0001.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p An overview of structuralist kinship theory and a criticism of permutation models is
          %ul
            %li#Sperber68
              Dan Sperber,
              %em Le structuralisme en anthropologie
              , pp.167-238 in Ducrot et al. ,
              %em Qu’est-ce que le structuralisme?
              , Seuil Paris 1968.
          %p Courrège’s seminal paper is easily accessible and eminently readable:
          %ul
            %li#Cour65
              Philippe Courrège,
              %em Un modèle mathématique des structures élémentaires de parenté
              , L’Homme
              %strong 5
              no.3-4 (1965) pp.248-290. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.persee.fr/doc/hom_0439-4216_1965_num_5_3_366751"}> link
              ; Engl. translation in Ballonoff (ed.),
              %em Genetics and Social Structure
              , Hutchinson&amp;Ross 1974)
          %p A useful overview and synthesis of the algebraic approach is
          %ul
            %li
              Franklin E. Tjon Sie Fat,
              %em Representing Kinship: Simple Models of Elementary Structures
              , PhD. Leiden University 1990.
          %p A visit to the library is richly rewarded by the clear and comprehensive exposition in the following outstanding monograph:
          %ul
            %li#Liu86
              Pin-Hsiung Liu,
              %em Foundations of Kinship Mathematics
              , Academica Sinica Nankang 1986.
          %p with companion volume by his collaborator
          %ul
            %li
              S. H. Gould,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A New System for the Formal Analysis of Kinship
          %p The category-theoretic point of view is sketched in
          %ul
            %li#Law99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> F. William Lawvere
                ,
                %em Kinship and Mathematical Categories
                , pp.411-425 in Jackendoff, Bloom, Wynn (eds.),
                %em Language, Logic, and Concepts
                , MIT Press Cambridge 1999.
            %li#LawSchan97
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> F. William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Schanuel"} Stephen H. Schanuel
                %em Conceptual Mathematics
                , Cambridge UP 1997. (pp.162-163)
          %p The following pursues an abstract general approach to kinship:
          %ul
            %li#Allen86
              N. J. Allen,
              %em Tetradic Theory: An Approach to Kinship
              , J. Anthr. Soc. Oxford
              %strong 17
              (1986) pp.87-109. (Revised reprint pp.221-235 in
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#PS04"} Parkin-Stone 2004
          %p The general role of group theory in culture is dicussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                P. Lucich,
                %em Beyond Formalism: Group Theory in the Symmetries of Culture
                , J. Math. Sociology
                %strong 16
                (1991) pp.221-264.
            %li
              %p
                P. Lucich,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Genealogical Symmetries: Rational Foundations of Australian Kinship
          %p A group-theoretic approach to Dravidian kinship and universal classification is proposed in
          %ul
            %li#Alm10
              Mauro W. Barbosa De Almeida,
              %em On the Structure of Dravidian Relationship Systems
              , MACT
              %strong 3
              no.1 (2010). (
              %a{:href => "http://mathematicalanthropology.org/pdf/BarbosadeAlmeida.0810.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A connection between Dravidian kinship terminology and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            is proposed in
          %ul
            %li#Vaz14
              Ruth M. Vaz,
              %em Relatives, Molecules and Particles
              , MACT
              %strong 7
              no. 1 (2014). (
              %a{:href => "http://mathematicalanthropology.org/pdf/RMVaz_MACT%200614.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p Kinship systems are studied from a mathematical perspective in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                John P. Boyd,
                %em The Algebra of Group Kinship
                , J. Math. Psychology
                %strong 6
                (1969) pp.139-167.
            %li
              %p
                John P. Boyd, John H. Haehl, Lee D. Sailer,
                %em Kinship and Inverse Semigroups
                , J. Math. Sociology
                %strong 2
                no.1 (1972) pp.37-61.
            %li
              %p
                Aimé Fuchs,
                %em Les structures de parenté: traitement mathématique
                , Revue Européenne des Sciences Sociales et Cahiers Vilfredo Pareto
                %strong 19
                (1981) pp.161-182.
            %li
              %p
                Alain Gottcheiner,
                %em On some Classes of Kinship Systems I: Abelian Systems
                , MACT
                %strong 2
                no. 4 (2008). (
                %a{:href => "http://mathematicalanthropology.org/Pdf/GottscheinerI_0908.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Alain Gottcheiner,
                %em On some Classes of Kinship Systems II: Nonabelian Systems
                , MACT
                %strong 2
                no. 5 (2008). (
                %a{:href => "http://mathematicalanthropology.org/Pdf/GottscheinerII_0908.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Labib Haddad, Yves Sureau,
                %em Les groupes, les hypergroupes et l’énigme des Murngin
                , JPAA
                %strong 87
                (1993) pp.221-235.
            %li
              %p
                Per Hage, Frank Harary,
                %em The Logical Structure of Asymmetric Marriage
                , L’Homme
                %strong 36
                no.139 (1996) pp.109-124. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.persee.fr/doc/hom_0439-4216_1996_num_36_139_370120"}> link
                )
            %li
              %p
                Frank Harary, Douglas R. White,
                %em P-Systems: A Structural Model for Kinship Studies
                , Connections
                %strong 24
                no.2 (2001) pp.35-46. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.insna.org/PDF/Connections/v24/2001_I-2-6.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Paul Jorion, Gisèle De Meur, Trudeke Vuyk,
                %em Le mariage Pende
                , L’Homme
                %strong 22
                no.1 (1982) pp.53-73. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.persee.fr/doc/hom_0439-4216_1982_num_22_1_368256"}> link
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claude+L%C3%A9vi-Strauss"}> Claude Lévi-Strauss
                , Georges Guilbaud,
                %em Système parental et matrimonial au Nord Ambrym
                , J. Société des Océanistes
                %strong 26
                no. 26 (1970) pp.9-32. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.persee.fr/doc/jso_0300-953x_1970_num_26_26_2281"}> link
                )
            %li
              %p
                Gisèle De Meur (ed.),
                %em New Trends in Mathematical Anthropology
                , Routledge London 1986.
            %li
              %p
                Gisèle De Meur, Alain Gottcheiner,
                %em Prescriptive Kinship Systems, Permutations, Groups, and Graphs
                , MACT
                %strong 1
                no. 1 (2000). (
                %a{:href => "http://mathematicalanthropology.org/Pdf/MACTdeMEUR1100.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Harrison C. White,
                %em An Anatomy of Kinship
                , Prentice-Hall Englewood Cliffs 1963.
          %p For related ideas see also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wyllis Bandler,
                %em Some Esomathematical Uses of Category Theory
                , pp.243-255 in Glir (ed.),
                %em Applied General Systems Research
                , Springer Heidelberg 1978.
            %li
              %p
                John P. Boyd,
                %em The Universal Semigroup of Relations
                , Social Networks
                %strong 2
                no.2 (1979) pp.91-117.
            %li
              %p
                John P. Boyd,
                %em Social Semigroups
                , George Mason UP 1991.
            %li
              %p
                François Lorrain,
                %em Réseaux sociaux et classifications sociales: Essai sur l ‘algèbre et la géométrie des structures sociales
                , Hermann Paris 1975.
            %li
              %p
                François Lorrain, Harrison C. White,
                %em Structural Equivalence of Individuals in Social Networks
                , J. Math. Sociology
                %strong 1
                (1971) pp.49-80.
            %li
              %p
                Oystein Ore,
                %em Sex in Graphs
                , Proc. AMS
                %strong 11
                (1960) pp.533-539. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1960-011-04/S0002-9939-1960-0117174-7/S0002-9939-1960-0117174-7.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Nina Otter, Mason J. Porter,
                %em A unified framework for equivalences in social networks
                , arXiv:2006.10733 (2020). (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.10733"}> abstract
                )
          %p
            First steps in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            can be undertaken with
            %a{:href => "#LawSchan97"} Lawvere-Schanuel (1997)
            whereas the level of sophistication then increases steadily from
          %ul
            %li#RRZ04
              M. La Palme Reyes,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+E.+Reyes"} G. E. Reyes
              %em Generic Figures and their Glueings
              , Polimetrica Milano 2004.
          %p to the standard textbook
          %ul
            %li#MM94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
              , Springer Heidelberg 1994.
          %p The result concerning essential localizations is in
          %ul
            %li#KL89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+M.+Kelly"}> G. M. Kelly
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"} F. W. Lawvere
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Complete Lattice of Essential Localizations
              %strong XLI
              (1989) 289-319 &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Kelly-Lawvere_EssentialLocalizations.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{M_2}
            is studied under the name
            %em category of autographs
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                R. Guitart,
                %em Autocategories: I. A common setting for knots and 2-categories
                , Cah. Top. Géom. Diff. Cat.
                %strong LV
                (2014) pp.66-82.
            %li
              %p
                R. Guitart,
                %em Autocategories: II. Autographic algebras
                , Cah. Top. Géom. Diff. Cat.
                %strong LV
                (2014) pp.151-160.
            %li
              %p
                R. Guitart,
                %em
                  Autocategories: III. Representations and expansions of previous examples , Cah. Top. Géom. Diff. Cat.
                  %strong LVIII
                  (2017) pp.67-81.
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  Quoted from C. Lévi-Strauss, D. Eribon,
                  %em De près et de loin
                  , Odile Jacob Paris (1990, p.78). See also the quotation at
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claude+L%C3%A9vi-Strauss"} Claude Lévi-Strauss
                  concerning his encounters with Hadamard and Weil during the preparation of his thesis in New York during WW2.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                  are called the
                  %em maternal
                  resp.
                  %em paternal
                  function in Courrège (1965).
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:3
                %p
                  so unconstrained indeed, that it might be better to think of the societies as being composed of ‘sections’ or ‘marriage classes’, or more generally of equivalence classes of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em classificatory kinship
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo ,
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,f
                  as the assignment of the marriage classes
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m(x),f(x)
                  of the respective parents of the individuals in the class
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                  in the spirit of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#Cour65"} Courrège (1965)
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:3", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:4
                %p
                  Again it makes more sense to consider
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                  to consist of two gender groups instead of two individuals.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:4", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:5
                %p
                  The Fox tribe in Lawvere’s home state Iowa indeed consists of two moieties called ‘Bear’ and ‘Wolf’ but they are patrilineal (
                  %a{:href => "#Liu86"}> Liu 1986
                  , p.21).
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:5", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
